# Elasticity, Surface Tension, and Viscosity: Mechanical Properties of Matter

> Bridges sway in wind, insects walk on ponds, and honey pours slowly.
> Three distinct properties of matter answer for all of these: solids
> resist deformation (elasticity), liquid surfaces shrink like stretched
> skin (surface tension), and fluid layers drag on each other
> (viscosity). This lesson assembles the three into one working toolkit.

## 1. Stress and Strain

When a body is deformed by an external force, internal restoring forces
appear. The restoring force per unit area is **stress**, and the
fractional change in dimensions is **strain** — the deformation itself.

| Type | Stress | Strain |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Tensile | $\frac{F}{A}$ (pull along length) | $\frac{\Delta L}{L}$ (elongation) |
| Bulk | $\frac{F}{A} = \Delta P$ (uniform squeeze) | $\frac{\Delta V}{V}$ (volume change) |
| Shear | $\frac{F}{A}$ (tangential) | $\theta$ (angular tilt) |

Stress has units of pressure ($\text{N/m}^2$ or pascal) while strain is
a pure ratio — dimensionless.

## 2. Hooke's Law and the Elastic Moduli

[definition]
Within the **elastic limit**, stress is directly proportional to
strain. The constant of proportionality is called the modulus of
elasticity of the material.
[/definition]

@math label="eq:hooke"
\text{Stress} = E \times \text{Strain}
@/math

Three moduli match the three deformations:

@math label="eq:young"
Y = \frac{F/A}{\Delta L / L} = \frac{FL}{A\,\Delta L}
@/math

@math label="eq:bulk"
B = -\frac{\Delta P}{\Delta V / V}
@/math

@math label="eq:rigidity"
G = \frac{F/A}{\theta}
@/math

[important]
Young's modulus $Y$ and the bulk modulus $B$ are defined only for
solids (liquids cannot sustain tensile or shear stress), while the
compressibility $\frac{1}{B}$ is most useful for gases and liquids.
Also, $Y$ is a **material** property: a steel wire and a steel girder
share the same $Y \approx 2 \times 10^{11}\ \text{Pa}$ however
different their shapes.
[/important]

**Elastic potential energy.** Stretching a wire stores energy in it,
just as stretching a spring does:

@math label="eq:elastic-energy"
U = \frac{1}{2} \times \text{stress} \times \text{strain} \times \text{volume}
= \frac{1}{2} \frac{(F/A)^2}{Y} \times \text{volume}
@/math

The stress-strain graph climbs linearly to the proportional limit,
bends through the elastic limit, and ends at the breaking point. The
area under the graph measures work done per unit volume.

## 3. Surface Tension and Surface Energy

[definition]
**Surface tension** $S$ is the force per unit length acting along a
liquid surface, or equivalently the work done to create unit area of
new surface. Its unit is $\text{N/m}$ (same as $\text{J/m}^2$).
[/definition]

Molecules at the surface miss half their attractions, so the surface
stores extra energy and behaves like a stretched elastic membrane —
always trying to shrink to minimum area. That is why raindrops are
nearly spherical (sphere = least area for a given volume) and a needle,
though denser than water, can float if laid gently.

**Excess pressure inside curved surfaces.** A curved liquid surface
needs a pressure difference to stay in equilibrium:

@math label="eq:drop-pressure"
\Delta P = \frac{2S}{R} \quad \text{(liquid drop)}
@/math

@math label="eq:bubble-pressure"
\Delta P = \frac{4S}{R} \quad \text{(soap bubble — two surfaces)}
@/math

**Capillary rise.** A tube of radius $r$ dipped in a wetting liquid
draws the liquid up to height $h$ where surface tension balances
weight:

@math label="eq:capillary"
h = \frac{2S\cos\theta}{r \rho g}
@/math

Here $\theta$ is the angle of contact. Water in glass has
$\theta \approx 0°$ (rises), mercury in glass has
$\theta \approx 140°$ (depressed). Thinner tubes give higher rises —
the principle by which plants lift water and ink climbs blotting paper.

## 4. Viscosity: Fluid Friction

[definition]
**Viscosity** is the resistance of a fluid to relative motion between
its layers. The viscous force is proportional to the area and the
velocity gradient — Newton's law of viscosity — and the constant
$\eta$ is the coefficient of viscosity.
[/definition]

@math label="eq:newton-viscosity"
F = -\eta A \frac{dv}{dy}
@/math

The SI unit of $\eta$ is $\text{Pa}\cdot\text{s}$ (older: poise). Gases
have tiny viscosities; engine oils have large ones. Unlike solids,
fluid flow obeys the layer picture: adjacent layers slide, and the
drag depends on speed — honey in winter illustrates how strongly
$\eta$ grows as temperature falls (for liquids, viscosity *decreases*
with temperature; for gases it *increases*).

**Poiseuille's equation** gives the volume flowing per second through
a narrow tube under pressure difference $\Delta P$:

@math label="eq:poiseuille"
Q = \frac{\pi R^4 \Delta P}{8 \eta L}
@/math

The dramatic fourth power of radius is why a slight narrowing of
arteries forces the heart to work much harder.

**Stokes' law and terminal velocity.** A small sphere of radius $r$
moving slowly through a fluid of viscosity $\eta$ feels a drag:

@math label="eq:stokes"
F = 6\pi \eta r v
@/math

A falling body accelerates until drag plus buoyancy equals weight;
then it falls at constant **terminal velocity**. For a sphere of
density $\rho$ in a fluid of density $\sigma$:

@math label="eq:terminal"
v_t = \frac{2 r^2 (\rho - \sigma) g}{9 \eta}
@/math

This is how raindrops arrive gently, how dust stays airborne for days,
and how Millikan weighed the electron with oil droplets.

## 5. Steady Flow, Turbulence, and Bernoulli's World

Viscous flow through pipes splits into two regimes. Below a critical
**Reynolds number** ($R_e = \frac{\rho v D}{\eta}$), flow is laminar —
smooth layers sliding past each other, obeying Poiseuille. Above it,
flow turns turbulent and resistance rises steeply with speed. Blood
flow in healthy arteries is laminar; plaques and narrowed valves push
it toward turbulence, which doctors hear as murmurs.

[example]
A bubble of air of diameter $2\ \text{mm}$ rises through water.
Using Stokes' law with the density difference $\rho - \sigma \approx
-1000\ \text{kg/m}^3$ (air far lighter), the terminal velocity comes
out a few centimetres per second — which is why champagne bubbles
leisurely parade upward in straight lines, then wobble when they grow
past the laminar limit.
[/example]

## 6. Quick Comparison Table

| Property | Symbol/Equation | Depends on | Typical question |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Young's modulus | $Y = \frac{FL}{A\Delta L}$ | Material only | Wire extension under load |
| Surface tension | $S$, drop: $\frac{2S}{R}$ | Liquid and interface | Excess pressure, capillary rise |
| Viscosity | $\eta$, $F = 6\pi\eta r v$ | Fluid and temperature | Terminal velocity, flow rate |
| Elastic energy | $\frac{1}{2} \times$ stress $\times$ strain $\times$ volume | Loading | Energy stored in a wire |

[summary]
Elasticity describes solids: within the elastic limit, stress is
proportional to strain, with Young's modulus $Y = \frac{FL}{A\Delta L}$
measuring stiffness and elastic energy $\frac{1}{2} \times$
stress $\times$ strain $\times$ volume stored on stretching. Surface
tension $S$ makes liquid surfaces contract, giving excess pressure
$\frac{2S}{R}$ in drops, $\frac{4S}{R}$ in soap bubbles, and capillary
rise $h = \frac{2S\cos\theta}{r\rho g}$. Viscosity $\eta$ resists
layer motion with $F = \eta A \frac{dv}{dy}$, drives Poiseuille's
$Q = \frac{\pi R^4 \Delta P}{8\eta L}$, and yields terminal velocity
$v_t = \frac{2r^2(\rho-\sigma)g}{9\eta}$ from Stokes' law. Three
properties, one lesson: how matter resists change — in shape, area,
and flow.
[/summary]
